---
name: nadia-fsx-check
version: 0.1.0
description: Runs nadia.fsx against the cases that raise with the raw std.fs primitives; exits non-zero if the contract changed.
---

# Contract check for `nadia.fsx`

```bash
ssc run src/fsx-check.ssc          # exit 0 = the contract holds
```

Each case here is one that **raises** when the raw `std.fs` primitive is called instead —
which is the whole reason the module exists. This side of the project has no test harness
(`BACKLOG.md` `ssc-test-harness`), so the contract runs as a script.

The checks use a directory that does not exist and one that does, and they cover the case
the miss actually happened on: a path that was there when the code was written and is gone
by the time the code runs.

[entriesOf, textOf, textOr, isDirSafe, isFileSafe](fsx.ssc)
[writeFile, mkdirs, deleteFile](std/fs.ssc)
[exit](std/os.ssc)

```scalascript
var failures = 0

def check(name: String, ok: Boolean): Unit =
  if ok then println("  ok   " + name)
  else
    println("  FAIL " + name)
    failures = failures + 1

val missing = "/tmp/nadia-fsx-check-does-not-exist"
val root    = "/tmp/nadia-fsx-check"
val file    = root + "/a.txt"
val empty   = root + "/empty.txt"

mkdirs(root)
writeFile(file, "hello")
writeFile(empty, "")

println("nadia.fsx — contract check")

// The four that raise on the raw primitives. This is the module's entire reason to exist.
check("entries of a missing directory are empty", entriesOf(missing).length == 0)
check("text of a missing file is None", textOf(missing).isEmpty)
check("textOr of a missing file is the fallback", textOr(missing, "d") == "d")
check("isDirSafe on a missing path is false", !isDirSafe(missing))
check("isFileSafe on a missing path is false", !isFileSafe(missing))

// And they must still tell the truth about things that DO exist — a total function that
// always answers "nothing" would pass every check above and be useless.
check("entries of a real directory are found", entriesOf(root).length == 2)
check("text of a real file is its content", textOf(file) == Some("hello"))
check("textOr of a real file ignores the fallback", textOr(file, "d") == "hello")
check("isDirSafe on a real directory is true", isDirSafe(root))
check("isFileSafe on a real file is true", isFileSafe(file))

// An EMPTY file is not a missing one. This is why textOf returns Option rather than "".
check("an empty file reads as Some(\"\"), not as None", textOf(empty) == Some(""))

// A directory is not a file and a file is not a directory — the predicates must not confuse
// the two, or `entriesOf(some/file.txt)` would raise again on the way in.
check("a file is not a directory", !isDirSafe(file))
check("a directory is not a file", !isFileSafe(root))
check("entries of a path that is a FILE are empty", entriesOf(file).length == 0)

// The measured case: the path was there when the code was written and is gone when it runs.
val doomed = root + "/doomed"
mkdirs(doomed)
check("a directory that exists has entries readable", isDirSafe(doomed))
deleteFile(doomed)
check("and once it is gone, the same call answers empty instead of raising",
      entriesOf(doomed).length == 0)

deleteFile(file)
deleteFile(empty)

println("")
if failures == 0 then
  println("contract holds")
  exit(0)
else
  println(failures.toString + " case(s) changed — docs/specs/total-fs.md and this file disagree")
  exit(1)
```
